{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012PmvkW3BjhkeAxR8kSRvaJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J5vyDboL5kNB8fPwN54fhV","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T062008/best/paper.tex","old_string":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[preprint]{neurips_2024}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}","new_string":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":565,"cache_read_input_tokens":61893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":565},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5fa804ce-a093-46e4-8164-2ae06f53b757","uuid":"fd9f87f5-0551-4e1b-9884-72f980397155"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J5vyDboL5kNB8fPwN54fhV","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T062008/best/paper.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5fa804ce-a093-46e4-8164-2ae06f53b757","uuid":"d9b224e6-b31a-4414-a1ae-b578f3eab03b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:43:54.005Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T062008/best/paper.tex","oldString":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[preprint]{neurips_2024}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}","newString":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}","originalFile":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[preprint]{neurips_2024}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{31\\% of MMLU Items Are Functionally Unstable:\\\\Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models Reveals\\\\Temporal Benchmark Fragility}\n\n\\author{\n  Anonymous Authors\n}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLarge Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: a score of 80\\% today measures the same construct as 80\\% one year ago. We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis---a standard psychometric method for detecting items whose difficulty shifts across examinee groups---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. We find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, a finding robust across effect-size thresholds (10--49\\%), multiple testing correction (BH removes 3/3{,}918 items), and model family de-duplication. Item-level decomposition shows domain capability improvement explains at most 7.6\\% of the instability. Crucially, instability does not threaten validity: removing all unstable items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$). We demonstrate that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$) and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR${}=0.000$), portable to GSM8K ($\\Delta$TPR${}=+0.818$). Item characterization reveals a mechanistic pattern: DIF direction anti-correlates with item difficulty ($\\rho = -0.597$)---hard items favor newer models while easy items erode, suggesting capability improvement concentrates on challenging content. Cross-benchmark validation on GSM1K shows DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$).\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWhen we report that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU by 5 points,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct---that each item's difficulty is stable across the two model cohorts being compared. In educational testing, this assumption is routinely verified through \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis, which detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\citep{holland1988differential}. If many items exhibit DIF, cross-cohort score comparisons become unreliable, even if aggregate rankings remain stable.\n\nDespite the centrality of temporal score comparability to LLM evaluation, no prior work has systematically tested it at scale. Existing contamination detection methods---Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting}, ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat}, n-gram overlap \\citep{li2024task}---focus on identifying \\emph{whether} models have seen benchmark items, but do not address \\emph{whether item functioning has changed} across model generations. These methods validate against web-overlap labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently for different model cohorts). We demonstrate that this distinction is fundamental.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{eisenstein2025metabench}---5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items---constituting the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our central finding is that \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across model generations, exhibiting large DIF (ETS Category~C, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This instability spans all 57 MMLU subjects, is robust to threshold choice, multiple testing correction, model family de-duplication, and cannot be explained by domain-level capability improvement (which accounts for $\\leq$7.6\\% of observed instability).\n\nA key framing principle guides our analysis: \\textbf{instability $\\neq$ invalidity}. DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all 31\\% unstable items), so instability does not threaten the benchmark's ability to rank models. But it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting, and this diagnostic information is scientifically valuable.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale.} We report the first large-scale DIF analysis of an LLM benchmark, finding 31.3\\% temporal instability that is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98\\% decoder-only). Item characterization reveals a mechanistic pattern: difficulty $\\times$ DIF direction interaction ($\\rho = -0.597$).\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} We demonstrate that web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), exposing a fundamental validation methodology gap affecting the entire contamination detection literature.\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We propose a $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR${}=0.000$), portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR${}=+0.818$), providing reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.} We show that DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z = -9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z = -157$), and that DIF items resist contamination-based gains (GSM1K $r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nSeveral methods detect whether LLMs have memorized benchmark items. Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting} thresholds on token-level log-likelihood outliers; ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on original vs.\\ paraphrased items; n-gram overlap methods \\citep{li2024task} use web-corpus co-occurrence as a contamination proxy. These methods share a common validation paradigm: they measure agreement with web-overlap or n-gram labels that indicate \\emph{global exposure}---whether an item exists on the public web---rather than \\emph{differential contamination}---whether an item functions differently across model cohorts. Our work demonstrates this distinction is consequential (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$).\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark integrity and temporal stability.}\nGrowing Pains \\citep{dutta2024growing} identifies anchor items for efficient LLM evaluation. LiveBench \\citep{white2024livebench} and similar efforts create continuously refreshed benchmarks to sidestep contamination. These approaches address the \\emph{symptom} (scores on static benchmarks may be inflated) but not the \\emph{diagnostic question} (which specific items changed and why). Our DIF analysis complements anchor-item approaches by identifying which anchors may be degrading.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning in psychometrics.}\nDIF analysis has been a cornerstone of educational testing for decades \\citep{holland1988differential}. The Mantel--Haenszel (MH) procedure \\citep{mantel1959statistical} is the most widely used method, with ETS classification \\citep{zieky1993practical} providing effect-size categories (A: negligible, B: moderate, C: large). Item-Focused Trees \\citep{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates. Logistic regression DIF \\citep{swaminathan1990detecting} captures non-uniform effects. We apply MH-DIF without modification; our contribution is the empirical findings at unprecedented scale, not algorithmic novelty.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}\nRecent work applies Item Response Theory (IRT) to LLM benchmarks \\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks,vania2021comparing}. These efforts focus on item parameter estimation and adaptive testing. We use IRT primarily for person-fit diagnostics ($L_z$ statistic) and note that unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$), motivating future multidimensional IRT work.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\\label{sec:mh}\n\nGiven a response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times J}$ where $N$ models respond to $J$ items, and a grouping variable $G \\in \\{\\text{reference}, \\text{focal}\\}$, the MH procedure tests whether item $j$ functions differently across groups after controlling for total score. Models are stratified by total score into $K$ strata. Within each stratum $k$, we form a $2 \\times 2$ table of group $\\times$ item response and compute:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the cell counts and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The log-odds ratio $\\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH})$ is classified using ETS criteria: Category~A ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.0$, negligible), Category~B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$, moderate), Category~C ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$, large). Positive $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ indicates the item favors the reference group; negative favors the focal group.\n\nFor temporal DIF, we define reference = models released before 2024 ($N_\\text{ref} = 1{,}253$) and focal = models released in 2024-H1 ($N_\\text{foc} = 656$). Total score on all 12,508 MMLU items serves as the matching variable.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisyn}\n\nTo validate DIF's ability to detect differential contamination under controlled conditions, we inject known contamination into the real response matrix. For a set of target items $\\mathcal{T}$ and focal-group models, we flip incorrect responses to correct with probability $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$, creating a ground truth where items in $\\mathcal{T}$ should exhibit DIF while non-target items should not. We measure:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0), \\quad \\Delta\\text{FPR}(p) = \\text{FPR}(p) - \\text{FPR}(0).\n\\end{equation}\nA valid detector should show monotonically increasing $\\Delta$TPR with $p$ and stable $\\Delta$FPR $\\approx 0$.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}\n\\label{sec:crosssubj}\n\nWhen the matching variable (total score) includes items being tested for DIF, injecting contamination inflates the matching variable for focal models, creating \\emph{matching variable contamination}. This produces spurious DIF on non-target items ($\\Delta$FPR $> 0$). We resolve this by using the total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the matching variable---e.g., when testing MMLU Anatomy items for DIF, we match on total score across the remaining 56 subjects. This cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR${}= 0.000$ across all tested subjects) while preserving detection power.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:exp}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll analyses use the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{eisenstein2025metabench}. For MMLU, this comprises 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items across 57 subjects. For GSM8K, 6,702 models $\\times$ 1,319 items.\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU (Claim~1)}\n\\label{sec:temporal}\n\n\\paragraph{Primary result.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} reports the temporal DIF analysis. Of 12,508 MMLU items, 31.3\\% (3,918 items) fall in ETS Category~C, indicating large differential functioning between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. An ability-quintile cohort split produces only 2.9\\% C-rate, and a random split yields 0.18\\%, confirming that the temporal signal is genuine and not a statistical artifact.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items). ETS categories: A (negligible), B (moderate), C (large, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$). Enrichment measures overlap with Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n\\toprule\nCohort Definition & $N_\\text{ref}$ & $N_\\text{foc}$ & A\\% & B\\% & C\\% & Enrichment \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs 2024) & 1{,}253 & 656 & 45.2 & 23.5 & 31.3 & 0.96 \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2{,}613 & 2{,}614 & 90.8 & 6.3 & 2.9 & 1.19 \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 2{,}613 & 2{,}614 & 99.6 & 0.2 & 0.18 & N/A \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe temporal--ability Jaccard index is 0.014, indicating that the temporal and ability DIF item sets are nearly disjoint: temporal instability identifies a fundamentally different set of items than ability-based differential functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:threshold} shows that the instability finding is robust across ETS thresholds. At the standard $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ threshold, 31.3\\% of items are flagged; at the most conservative threshold ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 2.5$), 10.2\\% (1,278 items) still exhibit large DIF. Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ has negligible impact, removing only 3 of 3,918 Category~C items at the standard threshold.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Threshold sensitivity analysis. The instability finding persists across all standard thresholds. BH FDR correction has near-zero impact due to the ETS dual requirement (effect size + significance).}\n\\label{tab:threshold}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n$|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ Threshold & ETS Class & C\\% & $N$ Items & BH Impact \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\geq 1.0$ & C & 48.9 & 6{,}109 & 0 removed \\\\\n$\\geq 1.5$ & C & 31.3 & 3{,}918 & 3 removed ($\\to$ 31.30\\%) \\\\\n$\\geq 2.0$ & Extreme C & 18.3 & 2{,}288 & 0 removed \\\\\n$\\geq 2.5$ & Extreme C & 10.2 & 1{,}278 & 0 removed \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Domain specificity.}\nInstability spans all 57 MMLU subjects (C\\% range: 20.7--48.4\\%, unweighted mean $\\approx$30\\%). The subject-level Spearman correlation between C\\% and accuracy improvement is $\\rho = 0.307$ ($p = 0.02$)---a modest ecological correlation. However, item-level decomposition (Table~\\ref{tab:decomposition}) reveals this overstates the relationship: domain-level accuracy improvement explains only 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C-rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$). The instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Instability decomposition: subject-level ecological correlation vs.\\ item-level logistic model. Domain improvement explains $\\leq$7.6\\% of observed temporal instability.}\n\\label{tab:decomposition}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llcl}\n\\toprule\nLevel & Metric & Value & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nSubject-level & Spearman $\\rho$ & 0.307 & Modest ecological correlation \\\\\nSubject-level & $p$-value & 0.02 & Significant but weak \\\\\nItem-level & Pseudo $R^2$ & 0.0007 & $<$1\\% variance explained \\\\\nItem-level & Contribution (pp) & 2.4 & Of 31.3\\% C\\%, only 2.4pp from domain improvement \\\\\nItem-level & Contribution (\\%) & 7.6 & 92.4\\% of instability is item-specific \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models). Excluding the single non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard = 1.0 between flagged item sets), eliminating architecture heterogeneity as a confound.\n\n\\paragraph{Model independence.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:model_indep} addresses the concern that fine-tuned variants and merges inflate DIF significance. Graduated de-duplication---capping models per family at $K \\in \\{1, 2, 5, 10, 20\\}$---shows that family-capped C\\% is \\emph{at or below} random-subsample C\\% at every cap level. Family structure does not inflate DIF; if anything, de-duplication slightly \\emph{reduces} it relative to random subsampling of equal size.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Model independence: graduated de-duplication. Family-capped C\\% $\\leq$ random subsample C\\% at every cap $K$, demonstrating that model family structure does not inflate DIF. $K=1$ is underpowered ($N < 80$).}\n\\label{tab:model_indep}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n\\toprule\nCap $K$ & $N$ Models & C\\% (family cap) & C\\% (random) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n20 & 447 & 29.1 & 30.9 & $-1.8$ \\\\\n10 & 283 & 24.3 & 27.5 & $-3.2$ \\\\\n5 & 146 & 11.7 & 16.9 & $-5.2$ \\\\\n2 & 66 & 2.1 & 5.8 & $-3.7$ \\\\\n1 & 33 & 0.17 & 0.84 & $-0.67^*$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\small $^*$Underpowered: $N < 80$ yields insufficient MH test power.}\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim~2)}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nThe temporal DIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.96---essentially no enrichment. Under the standard interpretation, this would suggest DIF fails to detect contamination. We argue the opposite: \\emph{enrichment $\\approx 1.0$ is expected} because web-overlap labels measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts).\n\nThis distinction has field-wide implications. All existing item-level contamination detectors---Min-K\\%, ConStat, n-gram methods---validate against web-overlap or similar labels. If these labels cannot distinguish differential effects from global exposure, then the validation evidence for all such detectors is fundamentally incomplete.\n\nWe provide direct evidence via our semi-synthetic framework (\\S\\ref{sec:semisyn_results}): injecting known differential contamination produces $\\Delta$TPR $= +0.508$ at injection rate $p = 0.5$, confirming DIF can detect differential contamination that web-overlap labels cannot capture.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim~3)}\n\\label{sec:semisyn_results}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU injection.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} shows the semi-synthetic injection results on MMLU using external (cross-subject) MH matching. $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically with injection rate, reaching $+0.674$ at $p = 1.0$. Crucially, FPR remains stable at 0.314 across all injection rates, confirming that contamination injection does not inflate false positives under cross-subject matching.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic injection results on MMLU (external MH matching). $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically with injection rate $p$ while FPR remains constant, validating the detection framework.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\nInjection Rate $p$ & TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$TPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & 0.314 & --- \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & 0.314 & $+0.222$ \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & 0.314 & $+0.508$ \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & 0.314 & $+0.664$ \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & 0.314 & $+0.674$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nFor comparison, standard (within-subject) MH matching suffers from matching variable contamination: FPR inflates from 0.298 to 0.616 at $p = 1.0$, rendering detection unreliable. Cross-subject matching eliminates this artifact ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all 5 tested subjects).\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark portability: GSM8K.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_dif} demonstrates the framework's portability. On GSM8K (6,702 models $\\times$ 1,319 items), temporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9\\%$ (vs.\\ sanity check 0.3\\%). Semi-synthetic injection achieves $\\Delta$TPR $= +0.818$ (standard MH) and $+0.517$ (external MH), confirming the methodology generalizes to single-domain math benchmarks.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic validation. The framework generalizes beyond MMLU to single-domain math benchmarks.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_dif}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Standard MH & External MH & Sanity \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% & 18.9 & 47.4 & 0.3 \\\\\n$N_\\text{ref}$ & 1{,}017 & 1{,}017 & 3{,}350 \\\\\n$N_\\text{foc}$ & 804 & 804 & 3{,}352 \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.10$) & 0.818 & 0.517 & N/A \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim~4)}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.}\nRemoving all 3,918 DIF-C items and re-ranking models on the remaining 8,590 stable items yields Spearman $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ with the full-benchmark ranking. This extreme rank-order stability reveals massive measurement redundancy: MMLU contains far more items than needed to produce stable model rankings.\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity ablation.}\nA natural concern is that DIF removal's low disruption is tautological---perhaps any ability-correlated selection strategy would produce similar results. Table~\\ref{tab:circularity} refutes this. We compare five item-removal strategies, each removing the same number of items (3,918). DIF removal ($z = -9.5$) is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy---16$\\times$ less disruptive than accuracy-gap removal ($z = -157$), despite 62.6\\% item overlap between the two sets. MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard = 0.12), confirming that DIF identifies ``items that changed across generations,'' not ``good items.''\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Circularity ablation: ranking disruption by removal strategy. All strategies remove the same number of items (3,918). DIF is the least disruptive non-random strategy, despite substantial item overlap with more disruptive strategies.}\n\\label{tab:circularity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nStrategy & $\\rho$ (full vs.\\ subset) & Disruption $z$ & Overlap w/ DIF \\\\\n\\midrule\nRandom baseline & 0.9985 & 0.0 & --- \\\\\nDIF-C removal & 0.997 & $-9.5$ & 100\\% \\\\\nHigh discrimination & 0.9958 & $-16.0$ & 22.1\\% \\\\\nHigh variance & 0.9831 & $-108$ & 41.3\\% \\\\\nAccuracy gap & 0.9712 & $-157$ & 62.6\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Item characterization.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:item_char} characterizes DIF-C items. They are moderately harder (Cohen's $d = -0.247$) and higher-discrimination ($d = 0.230$) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains ($\\chi^2$ $p = 0.32$). The key finding is the difficulty $\\times$ DIF direction interaction (Table~\\ref{tab:difficulty_direction}): DIF direction anti-correlates strongly with item difficulty ($\\rho = -0.597$). Among the hardest-decile DIF-C items, 88.7\\% favor the focal (2024) cohort; among the easiest-decile, 96.2\\% favor the reference (2023) cohort. This reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across model generations.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item characterization of DIF-C items ($N = 3{,}918$) vs.\\ non-DIF items ($N = 8{,}590$). DIF-C items are moderately harder and more discriminating, but uniformly distributed across domains.}\n\\label{tab:item_char}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nProperty & DIF-C Mean & Non-DIF Mean & Cohen's $d$ & $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDifficulty ($p$-value) & 0.58 & 0.65 & $-0.247$ & $<0.001$ \\\\\nDiscrimination ($r_\\text{pbis}$) & 0.34 & 0.28 & $+0.230$ & $<0.001$ \\\\\nDomain distribution & uniform & uniform & $\\chi^2$ $p = 0.32$ & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Difficulty $\\times$ DIF direction interaction ($\\rho = -0.597$). Hard items predominantly favor 2024 models (focal-favoring); easy items favor 2023 models (reference-favoring).}\n\\label{tab:difficulty_direction}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nDifficulty Decile & $N$ DIF-C & \\% Focal-favoring & \\% Reference-favoring \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 (easiest) & 287 & 3.8 & 96.2 \\\\\n2 & 312 & 8.3 & 91.7 \\\\\n5 (median) & 401 & 38.2 & 61.8 \\\\\n9 & 478 & 79.1 & 20.9 \\\\\n10 (hardest) & 512 & 88.7 & 11.3 \\\\\n\\midrule\nOverall & 3{,}918 & 43.9 & 56.1 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark validation: GSM1K contamination gap.}\nOn GSM8K, we correlate each model's DIF flag rate with its GSM1K contamination gap (the performance difference between GSM8K and the held-out GSM1K set). Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k} shows a significant negative correlation ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$): models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items, while DIF items capture genuine capability differences. The result survives controls for overall GSM8K accuracy (partial $r = -0.385$, $p = 0.027$), permutation testing ($p = 0.014$), and leave-one-out stability ($r$ range: $[-0.537, -0.374]$).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K contamination gap correlation ($N = 33$ models). DIF items anti-correlate with contamination-based gains. All confidence intervals exclude zero.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Statistic & Value & $p$ & 95\\% BCa CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson & $r$ & $-0.478$ & 0.005 & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ \\\\\nSpearman & $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & 0.020 & $[-0.687, -0.031]$ \\\\\nPartial $r$ & $r_\\text{partial}$ & $-0.385$ & 0.027 & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ \\\\\nPermutation & $p_\\text{perm}$ & --- & 0.014 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe emphasize appropriate caveats: $N = 33$ is small, the Pearson CI upper bound ($|r| = 0.155$) admits a small effect, and the Spearman CI upper bound ($|\\rho| = 0.031$) approaches zero. This result is suggestive cross-benchmark evidence, not a definitive claim. Replication with a larger matched sample is needed.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Implications for benchmark evaluation.}\nOur finding that 31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable has immediate practical implications. Benchmark maintainers should routinely apply DIF analysis when comparing model performance across temporal cohorts. The instability--invalidity distinction is critical: because MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), the 31\\% figure is a diagnostic signal rather than an alarm. It tells us \\emph{what changed}, not that ``the benchmark is broken.''\n\n\\paragraph{The difficulty--direction pattern.}\nThe strong interaction between item difficulty and DIF direction ($\\rho = -0.597$) suggests a mechanistic interpretation: as models improve, they disproportionately gain on hard items (which become focal-favoring) while the relative difficulty of easy items shifts (becoming reference-favoring). This is consistent with capability scaling concentrating gains on the frontier of model competence while altering the measurement properties of items that are ``too easy'' for newer models.\n\n\\paragraph{Growing Pains complementarity.}\nOur work complements the anchor-item paradigm of Growing Pains \\citep{dutta2024growing}: they identify anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic identifies which anchor items may be degrading over time. Combining the two approaches---using DIF to validate anchor stability before deployment---is a natural next step.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model misspecification.}\nUnidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure: 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, indicating poor person-fit under a unidimensional model. This reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality, not architecture heterogeneity (which is negligible at 99.98\\% decoder-only). MH-DIF is robust to IRT misspecification because it conditions on total score rather than latent trait estimates, but the misspecification motivates future work with multidimensional IRT or bifactor models. Our pilot bifactor analysis (1 general + 57 subject-specific factors) was killed after pre-checks showed within-subject C\\% = 38.1\\%, indicating temporal DIF penetrates the subject level. This negative result itself is informative: three structural improvements (matched ability, $\\theta$-conditioning, bifactor pre-check) all failed to reduce the baseline DIF rate, suggesting the instability is an empirical property of the benchmark, not a fixable methodological limitation.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplifications.}\nOur contamination injection mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct with probability $p$) is a simplification of real contamination patterns. Real contamination may produce partial credit, distractor-specific memorization, or format-dependent effects not captured by binary flipping. Cross-subject matching assumes approximate conditional independence of subject scores given ability, which may not hold for highly correlated subjects.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K sample size.}\nThe GSM1K contamination gap correlation ($N = 33$) has wide confidence intervals (Pearson upper bound $|r| = 0.155$). While all CIs exclude zero and the result survives leave-one-out analysis, it should be treated as suggestive rather than definitive.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.}\nPreliminary analysis suggests DIF is robust to reinforcement learning training paradigms (SFT vs.\\ alignment-RL vs.\\ reasoning-RL), with DIF retention ratio 0.97 and Jaccard similarity $\\geq 0.86$ across cohort compositions. However, only $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models were available, making this finding underpowered. Traditional detectors (Min-K\\%, ConStat) degrade substantially on RL models (AUROC drops of 0.31--0.33), while DIF remains stable ($\\Delta$AUROC $= -0.02$, $p = 0.42$). Validation with a larger reasoning-RL cohort is needed.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWe presented the first large-scale psychometric DIF analysis of an LLM benchmark, analyzing 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items. Our central finding---that 31\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations---is robust across effect-size thresholds, multiple testing correction, model family de-duplication, and cannot be explained by domain-level capability improvement. This instability is a diagnostic signal, not a validity threat: MMLU rankings remain extremely stable ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) after removing all unstable items.\n\nWe identified a fundamental gap in the field's contamination detection validation methodology---web-overlap ground truth cannot capture differential effects---and provided a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates FPR inflation and generalizes across benchmarks. Item characterization reveals that temporal instability follows a systematic pattern tied to item difficulty, providing mechanistic insight into how model capabilities reshape benchmark measurement properties across generations.\n\nOur work demonstrates that mature psychometric methods, applied at the unprecedented scale enabled by LLM benchmark ecosystems, can yield genuinely new empirical discoveries about the evaluation infrastructure on which the field relies.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n% References\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nJasper Dekoninck, Mark Niklas M{\\\"u}ller, and Martin Vechev.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Dutta et~al.(2024)]{dutta2024growing}\nAryaman Dutta, Abhinav Joshi, and Ashutosh Modi.\n\\newblock Growing pains: Understanding the impact of item overexposure in large language model benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.12697}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Eisenstein et~al.(2025)]{eisenstein2025metabench}\nJacob Eisenstein, et~al.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-benchmark of LLM evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the Mantel--Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nNathan Mantel and William Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nFelipe~Maia Polo, et~al.\n\\newblock {tinyBenchmarks}: Evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, et~al.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Swaminathan and Rogers(1990)]{swaminathan1990detecting}\nHariharan Swaminathan and H.~Jane Rogers.\n\\newblock Detecting differential item functioning using logistic regression procedures.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of Educational Measurement}, 27(4):361--370, 1990.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nGerhard Tutz and Moritz Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et~al.(2021)]{vania2021comparing}\nClara Vania, et~al.\n\\newblock Comparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[White et~al.(2024)]{white2024livebench}\nColin White, et~al.\n\\newblock {LiveBench}: A challenging, contamination-free LLM benchmark.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.19314}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993practical}\nMichael Zieky.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of {DIF} statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--364. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Results}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl_robust} reports DIF stability across training paradigms. DIF C\\% remains stable (30.5--31.1\\%) regardless of whether the focal cohort includes alignment-RL or reasoning-RL models. TOST equivalence testing confirms retention ($p = 0.008$; 95\\% CI $[0.93, 1.01]$). In contrast, traditional detectors degrade substantially on reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:trad_degrade}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF robustness to training paradigm. C\\% is stable across SFT, alignment-RL, and reasoning-RL cohorts.}\n\\label{tab:rl_robust}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nCohort Composition & $N$ & C\\% & Jaccard vs.\\ SFT & Retention \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 380 & 30.8 & 1.00 & 1.00 \\\\\nAlignment RL (DPO/ORPO) & 477 & 31.1 & 0.89 & 0.97 \\\\\nMixed reasoning RL & 393 & 30.5 & 0.86 & 0.97 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Traditional detector degradation on reasoning-RL models. Min-K\\% and ConStat AUROC drop substantially; DIF remains stable (DeLong $p = 0.42$).}\n\\label{tab:trad_degrade}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nDetector & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & $\\Delta$ & DeLong $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.68 & 0.35 & $-0.33$ & $<0.001$ \\\\\nConStat & 0.72 & 0.41 & $-0.31$ & $<0.001$ \\\\\nDIF (MH) & 0.93 & 0.91 & $-0.02$ & 0.42 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Non-Uniform DIF (Logistic Regression)}\n\\label{app:nonuniform}\n\nMH-DIF captures only uniform DIF (constant effect across ability levels). Table~\\ref{tab:nonuniform} reports logistic regression DIF on 9 subjects (1,787 items). MH and LR show 92.1\\% concordance for Category~C classification. Non-uniform DIF (interaction term $|\\beta_\\text{int}| \\geq 0.5$) affects an additional 24.8\\% of items, indicating our 31\\% uniform-only estimate is conservative.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Non-uniform DIF analysis via logistic regression (9 subjects, 1,787 items).}\n\\label{tab:nonuniform}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH--LR C concordance & 92.1\\% \\\\\nNon-uniform DIF (significance) & 38.7\\% \\\\\nNon-uniform DIF ($|\\beta_\\text{int}| \\geq 0.5$) & 24.8\\% \\\\\nSubjects tested & 9 \\\\\nItems tested & 1{,}787 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) treat model release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the arbitrary 2023/2024 binary split. IFT flags 28.7\\% of items vs.\\ MH's 31.3\\%, with Jaccard concordance of 0.72 (3,180 overlapping items). This confirms temporal instability is not an artifact of the cohort boundary choice.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH concordance. Both methods identify similar item sets, confirming robustness to cohort definition.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Items Flagged & Overlap w/ MH & Unique & Jaccard \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH-DIF & 3{,}918 & 3{,}180 & 738 & --- \\\\\nIFT & 3{,}592 & 3{,}180 & 412 & 0.72 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:bayesian} reports a simulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across successive administrations. Detection power improves from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.79 (four administrations) for $\\delta = 10$ effects, while FPR remains controlled at $\\approx$0.04. This suggests longitudinal DIF monitoring could substantially improve detection sensitivity.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power (simulation). Power improves with successive administrations while FPR remains controlled.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\n$N$ Administrations & Power ($\\delta{=}10$) & Power ($\\delta{=}14$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.42 & 0.68 & 0.041 \\\\\n2 & 0.58 & 0.82 & 0.039 \\\\\n3 & 0.71 & 0.91 & 0.037 \\\\\n4 & 0.79 & 0.95 & 0.038 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":["-\\documentclass{article}","-\\usepackage[preprint]{neurips_2024}","+\\documentclass[11pt]{article}","+\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}"," \\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}"," \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}"," \\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
